# Chapter 10 · Case Studies: Women and Governance in South Asia

> The syllabus names "**case studies of Sri Lanka, Pakistan, India, Bangladesh and Nepal**". No full question has yet been a pure case study, but comparisons strengthen every Topic 6 answer: Nepal's 33%, India's panchayats, Bangladesh's 50 seats and Sri Lanka's first woman prime minister are the evidence that design matters.

## 10.1  The South Asian paradox

South Asia has produced more women heads of government than almost any other region — **Sirimavo Bandaranaike**, **Indira Gandhi**, **Benazir Bhutto**, **Khaleda Zia**, **Sheikh Hasina**, **Chandrika Kumaratunga** — yet ordinary women's political participation remains low. Most of these leaders came from **political dynasties**: daughters or widows of assassinated or famous leaders. Their rise showed that women can lead, but it did not open politics to women in general.

::: fig timeline | Women heads of government and state in South Asia — selected firsts.
@layout vertical
1960: Sirimavo Bandaranaike (Sri Lanka) — the world's first woman prime minister
1966: Indira Gandhi becomes India's prime minister
1988: Benazir Bhutto (Pakistan) — first woman PM of a Muslim-majority country
1991: Khaleda Zia becomes Bangladesh's first woman prime minister
1994: Chandrika Kumaratunga — Sri Lanka's first woman president
1996: Sheikh Hasina's first term as Bangladesh's prime minister (again 2009–2024)
2007: Pratibha Patil — India's first woman president
2015: Bidya Devi Bhandari — Nepal's first woman president
2024: Harini Amarasuriya — Sri Lanka's third woman prime minister; Maryam Nawaz — Punjab's chief minister
:::

## 10.2  India

- **Suffrage:** women voted on equal terms from the first elections under the ==1950== Constitution.
- **Local quotas:** the **73rd and 74th Amendments** (==1992==) reserved **one-third** of seats and headships in panchayats and municipalities for women; many states have raised this to **50%**. More than a million women have been elected to local bodies.
- **Parliament:** women held about **14%** of the Lok Sabha after the ==2024== election (74 women).
- **The Women's Reservation Act:** after decades of failed bills (first introduced in 1996), Parliament passed the **Constitution (106th Amendment) Act, 2023** (*Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam*), reserving **one-third** of seats in the Lok Sabha and state assemblies for women for 15 years. It will operate only **after the next census and delimitation** — not before ==2029==.
- **Lessons:** rotating reserved constituencies allow **direct election** of women; local quotas produced evidence of policy impact (Chattopadhyay and Duflo); but "**sarpanch-pati**" (husband of the woman head) proxies are a known problem.

## 10.3  Bangladesh

- **Leadership:** two women — **Khaleda Zia** (BNP) and **Sheikh Hasina** (Awami League) — alternated as prime minister for most years from ==1991== to ==2024==. Hasina's government fell in **August 2024** after mass student-led protests.
- **Reserved seats:** **15** seats in ==1972==, **30** in ==1979==, a lapse in the early 2000s, **45** in ==2004== and **50** since ==2011==, filled by parties in proportion to their seats — similar to Pakistan.
- **2026 election:** of **85** women candidates, only **7** were directly elected; with the 50 reserved seats, women held **57** of **350** seats.
- **Local government:** since ==1997== three seats in each Union Parishad are reserved for women and **directly elected**, bringing tens of thousands of women into local politics.
- **Lessons:** women at the top did not translate into women on general seats; indirect reserved seats share Pakistan's weaknesses; directly elected local seats work better.

## 10.4  Sri Lanka

- **Suffrage:** **universal adult franchise** in ==1931== under the Donoughmore Constitution — among the first in Asia.
- **Leadership:** **Sirimavo Bandaranaike** (==1960==), the world's first woman prime minister, after her husband's assassination; her daughter **Chandrika Kumaratunga**, president (==1994–2005==); **Harini Amarasuriya**, prime minister from ==2024==.
- **Parliament:** despite high female literacy, women held only about **5%** of seats for decades. A record **22** women entered the 225-member parliament in ==2024==.
- **Local quota:** a **25%** quota for women in local councils, first applied in ==2018==, raised women's local representation from about 2% to over 20%.
- **Lessons:** education alone does not produce representation; **quotas** brought the leap.

## 10.5  Nepal

- **Constitution 2015:** requires that at least **one-third** of members of the federal parliament be women, and that the president and vice-president be of different sexes or communities; in each local body the head or deputy must include a woman, and wards must include women and **Dalit** women.
- **Mixed electoral system:** first-past-the-post seats plus **proportional representation** lists. Parties meet the 33% mainly through **PR lists**, where women are more than half of nominees.
- **2026 election:** women won **96 of 275** House seats (about **35%**) — but only **14** through FPTP.
- **Local level:** the ==2017== local elections brought over **14,000** women into office, about **41%** of local representatives, many of them Dalit women.
- **Leadership:** **Bidya Devi Bhandari** became the first woman president (==2015==); **Sushila Karki**, a former chief justice, became the first woman (interim) prime minister in ==2025==.
- **Lessons:** constitutional quotas with **PR lists** and **intersectional** rules (Dalit women) produce the region's highest representation; but women rarely win direct seats and are often deputies rather than heads.

## 10.5a  Pakistan as a case study

The syllabus lists Pakistan among the case studies. In one paragraph, Pakistan's story is this:

- **Firsts at the top:** Fatima Jinnah challenged a military ruler in ==1965==; **Benazir Bhutto** became the first woman prime minister of a Muslim-majority country (==1988==); **Maryam Nawaz** became Punjab's chief minister (==2024==).
- **A quota that came and went:** 10 seats (==1956==), 6 (==1962==), 10 (==1973==), 20 (==1985==), none in the 1990s, then **60** seats and **33%** local seats under Musharraf (==2000–02==).
- **Laws made by women:** the Women's Parliamentary Caucus (==2008==) and the pro-women laws of 2010–2016.
- **Persistent gaps:** a voter registration gap of ==9.94 million==, turnout of 43% against 52%, only **11** women on general National Assembly seats (==2024==), **no** women federal ministers (==2025==), and women legislators dependent on party leaders.

Compared with its neighbours, Pakistan sits in the **middle**: ahead of India and Sri Lanka in women's share of parliament, behind Nepal, and similar to Bangladesh in relying on indirectly filled reserved seats.

## 10.6  Pakistan in comparison

::: table | Women and governance in South Asia compared.
@widths 2.6,3.6,3.8,4
Country | National quota | Women in lower house (latest) | Distinctive feature
Pakistan | 60 reserved seats (2002); 5% tickets (2017) | About one-fifth | Indirect reserved seats; 10% turnout rule
India | One-third (2023 Act, from about 2029) | About 14% (2024) | One-third local quota since 1992, directly elected
Bangladesh | 50 reserved seats (2011) | 57 of 350 (2026) | Two women PMs for three decades
Sri Lanka | No national quota; 25% local quota | 22 of 225 (2024) | World's first woman PM (1960)
Nepal | One-third by constitution (2015) | 96 of 275 (2026) | PR lists; Dalit women's seats locally
:::

